Here's how to wire it up:

```csharp
// MauiProgram.cs
public static class MauiProgram
{
    public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
    {
        var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
        builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

        // HttpClient factory for the API base address
        builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IDataService, DataService>(client =>
        {
            client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com/");
        });

        // ViewModels — transient so each navigation gets fresh state
        builder.Services.AddTransient<MainViewModel>();
        builder.Services.AddTransient<DetailViewModel>();

        // Pages — transient to pair 1:1 with their ViewModel instance
        builder.Services.AddTransient<MainPage>();
        builder.Services.AddTransient<DetailPage>();

        return builder.Build();
    }
}
```

**Why these lifetimes?**

| Registration | Lifetime | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `DataService` | **Singleton** (via `AddHttpClient`) | Holds an `HttpClient` managed by the factory. One instance avoids socket exhaustion and lets you share auth tokens/headers app-wide. |
| `MainViewModel` / `DetailViewModel` | **Transient** | Each navigation should start with clean state. A singleton VM would leak stale data between navigations. |
| `MainPage` / `DetailPage` | **Transient** | Pages are tied to their VM; a new page + new VM per navigation avoids UI state bugs. |

**Key takeaway:** services that manage connections or expensive resources → singleton. UI-layer objects (pages/VMs) that represent a single user interaction → transient. Use scoped only if you have a well-defined scope (e.g., a per-request boundary), which MAUI doesn't provide by default.
